WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

NOTE Module 6: Seminar narrated walkthrough. Generated from the
narration script and per-segment audio durations (0.7 s gap per slide).

s01-001
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.462
Welcome to Module 6 of Claude Code
for Accounting Research.

s01-002
00:00:03.512 --> 00:00:06.533
This one is a seminar on AI,
the production of knowledge,

s01-003
00:00:06.583 --> 00:00:09.096
and the academic research market.

s01-004
00:00:09.146 --> 00:00:10.563
There's no lab and nothing to install,

s01-005
00:00:10.613 --> 00:00:13.230
so this video walks
through the framing slides.

s02-006
00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:15.973
So start with what you built.

s02-007
00:00:16.023 --> 00:00:20.645
In Module 2 you built an R&amp;D
intensity panel from the SEC's Financial

s02-008
00:00:20.695 --> 00:00:23.502
Statement Data Sets,
in an eighty-minute lab.

s02-009
00:00:23.552 --> 00:00:27.435
Around 2019, that was about
a week of an RA's time.

s02-010
00:00:27.485 --> 00:00:31.736
Module 3, the EDGAR risk-factor
pipeline and a reusable skill,

s02-011
00:00:31.786 --> 00:00:35.924
was also an eighty-minute lab,
against two to three weeks for an RA.

s02-012
00:00:35.974 --> 00:00:39.451
Module 4, a filtered, verified Compustat
pull, took the same eighty minutes,

s02-013
00:00:39.501 --> 00:00:42.714
against several days.

s02-014
00:00:42.764 --> 00:00:46.313
Module 5, a hardened repo with a paper
trail and git, took about three hours,

s02-015
00:00:46.363 --> 00:00:49.762
against days of audit work.

s02-016
00:00:49.812 --> 00:00:52.616
That speed-up raises a
question that isn't technical,

s02-017
00:00:52.666 --> 00:00:54.810
and this module is about that question.

s03-018
00:00:55.560 --> 00:01:00.662
In Module 1 you saw the research pipeline:
idea, data, measure, analysis, paper.

s03-019
00:01:00.712 --> 00:01:02.181
Here it's a harder map.

s03-020
00:01:02.231 --> 00:01:07.295
Each stage is plotted by how automatable
it is and how much of the paper's value

s03-021
00:01:07.345 --> 00:01:08.003
it carries.

s03-022
00:01:08.053 --> 00:01:11.839
Most of this course
worked in the upper left,

s03-023
00:01:11.889 --> 00:01:16.016
execution work an agent does
faster than you and about as well.

s03-024
00:01:16.066 --> 00:01:19.338
The upper right is identification,
contribution, and interpretation,

s03-025
00:01:19.388 --> 00:01:22.467
where a paper succeeds or fails.

s03-026
00:01:22.517 --> 00:01:23.395
The line between the two moves.

s03-027
00:01:23.445 --> 00:01:27.553
Measurement construction,
which you worked on in Module 3,

s03-028
00:01:27.603 --> 00:01:30.520
has been moving toward
the automatable side.

s03-029
00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:33.956
So, on your own, place your
dissertation's stages on this map,

s03-030
00:01:34.006 --> 00:01:36.119
and don't discuss it yet.

s03-031
00:01:36.169 --> 00:01:39.430
Block 1 opens with two volunteers
defending their placement.

s04-032
00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:42.090
A paper is an artifact.

s04-033
00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:45.622
Once it exists, it's
transferable and cheap to copy.

s04-034
00:01:45.672 --> 00:01:50.157
Your understanding is capital,
and you only build it by doing the work.

s04-035
00:01:50.207 --> 00:01:52.541
For most of the profession's
history the two came bundled,

s04-036
00:01:52.591 --> 00:01:56.030
so the paper was good
evidence about the researcher.

s04-037
00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:57.287
Agentic AI unbundles them.

s04-038
00:01:57.337 --> 00:02:01.474
The evidence on what that
does to learning is mixed.

s04-039
00:02:01.524 --> 00:02:04.040
Bastani and coauthors gave
high-school math students GPT-4.

s04-040
00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:06.717
Practice grades went
up forty-eight percent.

s04-041
00:02:06.767 --> 00:02:07.843
Then they took it away,

s04-042
00:02:07.893 --> 00:02:13.450
and those students scored seventeen percent
worse than students who never had it.

s04-043
00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:17.364
A version built as a tutor, with
safeguards, largely removed that harm.

s04-044
00:02:17.414 --> 00:02:19.170
That's novices.

s04-045
00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:22.594
Budzyń and coauthors looked
at experienced endoscopists.

s04-046
00:02:22.644 --> 00:02:24.522
After their clinics brought in AI,

s04-047
00:02:24.572 --> 00:02:28.620
the doctors' detection rate on colonoscopies
done without the AI fell from about

s04-048
00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:31.280
twenty-eight percent to about twenty-two.

s04-049
00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:34.421
That's a before-and-after
comparison with no control group,

s04-050
00:02:34.471 --> 00:02:36.725
so the design is worth arguing about.

s04-051
00:02:36.775 --> 00:02:39.753
The personal audit is
the test from Module 5:

s04-052
00:02:39.803 --> 00:02:42.370
can you discuss the
finding without the LLM?

s05-053
00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:46.869
Block 2 is about provenance:
who or what produced a result,

s05-054
00:02:46.919 --> 00:02:49.406
and who's accountable for it.

s05-055
00:02:49.456 --> 00:02:53.966
The quote on the slide is attributed
to a 1979 IBM training slide,

s05-056
00:02:54.016 --> 00:02:56.644
though the original
has never been found...

s05-057
00:02:56.694 --> 00:02:58.396
a computer can never be held accountable.

s05-058
00:02:58.446 --> 00:03:00.972
In 2023, Nature's editors set two rules,

s05-059
00:03:01.022 --> 00:03:06.059
and the AEA and the American Accounting
Association have since adopted versions

s05-060
00:03:06.109 --> 00:03:07.090
of the same two.

s05-061
00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:09.987
No language model gets
credited as an author,

s05-062
00:03:10.037 --> 00:03:15.272
because authorship carries
accountability a model can't take on.

s05-063
00:03:15.322 --> 00:03:17.135
And any use of these
tools has to be disclosed.

s05-064
00:03:17.185 --> 00:03:22.679
The second rule is where the profession
is putting its practical effort now:

s05-065
00:03:22.729 --> 00:03:26.282
disclosure policies, audit
trails, and verification.

s05-066
00:03:26.332 --> 00:03:29.950
You built that kind of documentation
in Module 5, with the paper trail.

s06-067
00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:34.443
This slide is a case study,
and it's labeled that way on purpose.

s06-068
00:03:34.493 --> 00:03:37.429
We don't cite this paper
as evidence for anything.

s06-069
00:03:37.479 --> 00:03:43.513
In 2024 a working paper claimed
that a randomized rollout of an AI

s06-070
00:03:43.563 --> 00:03:47.756
materials-discovery tool led researchers
to discover 44% more new materials.

s06-071
00:03:47.806 --> 00:03:50.818
It circulated to acclaim
from prominent economists,

s06-072
00:03:50.868 --> 00:03:54.682
and then MIT said it had no confidence
in the provenance, reliability,

s06-073
00:03:54.732 --> 00:03:59.545
or validity of the data, and asked
for the paper to be withdrawn.

s06-074
00:03:59.595 --> 00:04:02.226
The author is no longer at MIT.

s06-075
00:04:02.276 --> 00:04:06.573
So the field's provenance checks
failed before AI was writing papers.

s06-076
00:04:06.623 --> 00:04:09.653
Accounting has its own case,
of a different kind. Kim, Muhn,

s06-077
00:04:09.703 --> 00:04:14.679
and Nikolaev's 2024 paper on GPT-4
and financial statement analysis was

s06-078
00:04:14.729 --> 00:04:16.834
withdrawn by its authors in 2025,

s06-079
00:04:16.884 --> 00:04:21.734
after a co-author trying to replicate
it found inconsistencies in the data.

s06-080
00:04:21.784 --> 00:04:26.901
They describe the withdrawal as
temporary while they review the findings.

s06-081
00:04:26.951 --> 00:04:28.993
There, the checking worked.

s06-082
00:04:29.043 --> 00:04:33.371
The open question for Block 2 is whether
AI makes that machinery stronger,

s06-083
00:04:33.421 --> 00:04:37.699
through audit trails and replication
at near-zero cost, or weaker,

s06-084
00:04:37.749 --> 00:04:40.090
through volume and plausibility at scale.

s07-085
00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:43.096
Block 3 is about the market.

s07-086
00:04:43.146 --> 00:04:47.061
Placement, tenure, prestige,
and salary all clear through publications,

s07-087
00:04:47.111 --> 00:04:52.196
so papers are the currency, and they've
always been expensive to produce.

s07-088
00:04:52.246 --> 00:04:55.480
The readings measure what
happens when that cost falls.

s07-089
00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:59.431
Kusumegi and coauthors find that
researchers who adopt LLMs post somewhere

s07-090
00:04:59.481 --> 00:05:03.616
between twenty-four and
eighty-nine percent more papers,

s07-091
00:05:03.666 --> 00:05:07.280
and that polished prose stops
being a sign of a good paper.

s07-092
00:05:07.330 --> 00:05:11.705
Hao, Xu, Li, and Evans look
at a different kind of AI use:

s07-093
00:05:11.755 --> 00:05:14.745
natural-science papers
that apply AI methods,

s07-094
00:05:14.795 --> 00:05:17.162
mostly machine learning and deep learning.

s07-095
00:05:17.212 --> 00:05:21.719
Scientists whose work uses AI publish
about three times as many papers,

s07-096
00:05:21.769 --> 00:05:24.832
while the range of topics
the field covers narrows.

s07-097
00:05:24.882 --> 00:05:26.574
Those data are observational,

s07-098
00:05:26.624 --> 00:05:31.115
but they raise the question of whether what
pays the individual can cost the field.

s07-099
00:05:31.165 --> 00:05:33.033
The figure follows that logic.

s07-100
00:05:33.083 --> 00:05:36.061
Production cost falls,
paper volume goes up,

s07-101
00:05:36.111 --> 00:05:39.409
and referee time and reader
attention don't scale with it.

s07-102
00:05:39.459 --> 00:05:44.086
So the market reprices what attention
still depends on: taste in questions,

s07-103
00:05:44.136 --> 00:05:47.110
data access, credibility,
and accountability.

s08-104
00:05:47.860 --> 00:05:51.181
Here's how the discussion runs
when the seminar meets as a group.

s08-105
00:05:51.231 --> 00:05:55.530
Three blocks of about thirty minutes each,
with a five-minute stretch between them.

s08-106
00:05:55.580 --> 00:06:01.486
Each block starts with two-minute position
statements from debaters assigned in

s08-107
00:06:01.536 --> 00:06:03.354
advance, then open discussion,
then I synthesize.

s08-108
00:06:03.404 --> 00:06:07.545
Several people will be defending
positions they don't actually hold,

s08-109
00:06:07.595 --> 00:06:08.824
and that's part of the exercise.

s08-110
00:06:08.874 --> 00:06:11.102
If you're working on your own,

s08-111
00:06:11.152 --> 00:06:14.150
the module page turns each block
into a short writing exercise.

s08-112
00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:19.252
The ground rule is the same either way:
no catastrophizing and no dismissing.

s08-113
00:06:19.302 --> 00:06:22.763
If you raise a provocation,
pair it with its counterargument.

s08-114
00:06:22.813 --> 00:06:25.330
And the memo is due one
week after the seminar.

s09-115
00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:31.472
Block 1 asks: if AI produces the artifact,
what happens to the learning?

s09-116
00:06:31.522 --> 00:06:35.795
We start from the placement you just did,
with two volunteers defending theirs.

s09-117
00:06:35.845 --> 00:06:40.582
Then the question is which pipeline
stages build intuition worth protecting.

s09-118
00:06:40.632 --> 00:06:42.746
The accounting parallel is
AI-assisted audit judgment.

s09-119
00:06:42.796 --> 00:06:44.210
There's also the institutional version.

s09-120
00:06:44.260 --> 00:06:50.366
If researchers no longer have to build
the capital to produce the artifact,

s09-121
00:06:50.416 --> 00:06:52.383
does the profession keep
building it on purpose,

s09-122
00:06:52.433 --> 00:06:56.595
the way we still teach proofs to
students who'll never prove anything new?

s09-123
00:06:56.645 --> 00:07:01.068
The block closes on this question:
is PhD training a production process,

s09-124
00:07:01.118 --> 00:07:03.150
or a transformation of the student?

s10-125
00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:08.526
Block 2 asks whether it matters
who or what produced a result.

s10-126
00:07:08.576 --> 00:07:12.428
Take two identical extraction
results from the Module 3 pipeline.

s10-127
00:07:12.478 --> 00:07:15.322
An RA hand-coded one over three weeks.

s10-128
00:07:15.372 --> 00:07:18.593
The other came from a Claude session
with a full audit trail: cached inputs,

s10-129
00:07:18.643 --> 00:07:23.413
a quality report, committed code,
and a stratified human audit sample.

s10-130
00:07:23.463 --> 00:07:25.323
Are they equivalent?

s10-131
00:07:25.373 --> 00:07:28.841
A spotless audit trail can
still deliver a wrong estimate.

s10-132
00:07:28.891 --> 00:07:29.955
Module 5 had that example,

s10-133
00:07:30.005 --> 00:07:35.489
where DECISIONS.md claimed a robustness
check the commit history didn't contain.

s10-134
00:07:35.539 --> 00:07:40.020
A ten-minute segment then asks what's
being verified when a second model reviews

s10-135
00:07:40.070 --> 00:07:41.362
the first model's code,

s10-136
00:07:41.412 --> 00:07:45.691
since correlated training plausibly
produces correlated blind spots.

s10-137
00:07:45.741 --> 00:07:49.770
The block closes on the retraction
question: stronger or weaker?

s11-138
00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:53.141
Block 3 is returns to research.

s11-139
00:07:53.191 --> 00:07:56.523
You built the Module 4 pipeline
in an eighty-minute lab.

s11-140
00:07:56.573 --> 00:07:59.808
Now suppose every
accounting PhD student can.

s11-141
00:07:59.858 --> 00:08:04.121
What does the JAR submission queue look
like in five years? Who referees it?

s11-142
00:08:04.171 --> 00:08:07.192
What happens to the signal
value of a publication?

s11-143
00:08:07.242 --> 00:08:09.585
The candidates for what stays
scarce are question taste,

s11-144
00:08:09.635 --> 00:08:15.553
data access and institutional knowledge,
credibility, and accountability.

s11-145
00:08:15.603 --> 00:08:17.536
Then there's homogenization.

s11-146
00:08:17.586 --> 00:08:20.234
If everyone's referee reports
pass through the same models,

s11-147
00:08:20.284 --> 00:08:23.043
does voice still signal anything?

s11-148
00:08:23.093 --> 00:08:26.953
The block closes with each of you
naming one input you think stays scarce,

s11-149
00:08:27.003 --> 00:08:29.590
and that answer is where your memo starts.

s12-150
00:08:30.340 --> 00:08:34.623
The memo is titled What I will
still be paid for in 2036.

s12-151
00:08:34.673 --> 00:08:37.466
It's one page, due a
week after the seminar.

s12-152
00:08:37.516 --> 00:08:41.279
Commit to specific parts of the research
production function that you think stay

s12-153
00:08:41.329 --> 00:08:42.198
human.

s12-154
00:08:42.248 --> 00:08:46.501
Defend that with at least one assigned
reading and one experience from the build

s12-155
00:08:46.551 --> 00:08:46.987
modules.

s12-156
00:08:47.037 --> 00:08:51.484
And name one concrete investment you'll
make in your remaining PhD years.

s12-157
00:08:51.534 --> 00:08:55.215
Grading is credit or no credit
on defended specificity.

s12-158
00:08:55.265 --> 00:08:59.832
An optimist and a pessimist can both
earn credit, and a vague memo can't.

s12-159
00:08:59.882 --> 00:09:03.744
AI help is fine everywhere in this
course except the argument of this memo,

s12-160
00:09:03.794 --> 00:09:08.131
which has to be your own, and you
disclose what you used in one line.

s12-161
00:09:08.181 --> 00:09:09.330
See you at the seminar.
